### What is GenerationType.IDENTITY?

IDENTITY relies on the database's auto-increment feature. The column is defined as SERIAL/IDENTITY on PostgreSQL or AUTO_INCREMENT on MySQL. The database assigns the value during the INSERT. The id is not known until the row is inserted, which means Hibernate CANNOT do JDBC batch inserts because it must execute each INSERT individually to read back the generated key.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Entity
@Table(name = "accounts")
public class Account {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @Column(name = "holder_name")
    private String holderName;

    @Column(name = "balance", precision = 15, scale = 2)
    private BigDecimal balance;
}
```

```text
IDENTITY behaviour:
- INSERT INTO accounts (holder_name, balance) VALUES ('Rajesh', 150000.00)
- Database assigns id = 1
- Hibernate reads back the generated id immediately
- Cannot batch: each INSERT is a separate round trip
- Good for: MySQL where sequences are not available (before 8.0)
```

### What is GenerationType.SEQUENCE?

SEQUENCE uses a database sequence object. Hibernate asks the sequence for the next value and, with allocationSize, pre-allocates a block of ids in one call. This keeps batch inserts working and reduces round trips. It is the preferred strategy on PostgreSQL and Oracle.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Entity
@Table(name = "accounts")
public class Account {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.SEQUENCE, generator = "acc_seq")
    @SequenceGenerator(name = "acc_seq", sequenceName = "account_seq", allocationSize = 50)
    private Long id;

    @Column(name = "holder_name")
    private String holderName;

    @Column(name = "balance", precision = 15, scale = 2)
    private BigDecimal balance;
}
```

```text
SEQUENCE behaviour:
- SELECT nextval('account_seq')  -> returns 50 (allocationSize=50)
- Hibernate assigns ids 1-50 in memory without hitting DB again
- 50 INSERTs can be batched in one JDBC batch call
- Next call: SELECT nextval('account_seq') -> returns 100, assigns 51-100
- Best performance for bulk inserts
```

### What is GenerationType.TABLE?

TABLE emulates a sequence using a dedicated table that stores the last-used value per entity. Hibernate reads and increments that row under a lock. It is portable to any database but the slowest option because the row lock serializes id generation.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;

@Entity
public class Account {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.TABLE, generator = "acc_tab")
    @TableGenerator(name = "acc_tab", table = "id_keys",
            pkColumnName = "key_name", valueColumnName = "next_val",
            allocationSize = 50)
    private Long id;
}
```

```text
TABLE behaviour:
- SELECT next_val FROM id_keys WHERE key_name = 'Account' FOR UPDATE
- UPDATE id_keys SET next_val = next_val + 50 WHERE key_name = 'Account'
- Row lock serializes id generation -> slowest strategy
- Use ONLY when the database has no sequence support
```

### What is GenerationType.UUID?

UUID generates a 128-bit UUID in the application with no database round trip. It is ideal for distributed systems where multiple nodes need to generate ids without coordination.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;

@Entity
public class Payment {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.UUID)
    private java.util.UUID id;

    private String fromAccount;
    private String toAccount;
    private java.math.BigDecimal amount;
}
```

```text
UUID behaviour:
- Application generates UUID, no database round trip
- Id known before persist -> can return it immediately
- Ideal as idempotency key for distributed payment service
- CAUTION: random UUIDv4 as clustered PK causes index fragmentation
```

### How do the five strategies compare?

| Aspect | IDENTITY | SEQUENCE | TABLE | AUTO | UUID |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Id source | DB auto-increment | DB sequence | Key table | Provider picks | Application |
| Batching | Not possible | Yes (allocationSize) | Yes (allocationSize) | Depends | Yes |
| Round trips | 1 per INSERT | 1 per block | 1 per block (locked) | Depends | 0 |
| Id known before flush | No | Yes | Yes | Depends | Yes |
| Distributed | No | No (single DB) | No (single DB) | Depends | Yes |
| Index impact | Sequential (good) | Sequential (good) | Sequential (good) | Depends | Random v4 (bad) |

```java
// IDENTITY: one round trip per INSERT (no batching)
@Id @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
private Long id;

// SEQUENCE: one round trip per block of 50 (batching works)
@Id @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.SEQUENCE, generator = "seq")
@SequenceGenerator(name = "seq", sequenceName = "account_seq", allocationSize = 50)
private Long id;

// UUID: zero round trips (app-generated)
@Id @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.UUID)
private java.util.UUID id;
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is using IDENTITY on a high-volume batch import, which makes every INSERT a separate round trip. A second mistake is using random UUIDv4 as a clustered primary key, causing index fragmentation.

```java
// WRONG: IDENTITY for batch import -- no batching possible
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;

@Entity
public class Transaction {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;
    // 100,000 INSERTs = 100,000 round trips -- very slow!
}
```

```java
// CORRECT: SEQUENCE for batch import -- 50 ids per round trip
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;

@Entity
public class Transaction {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.SEQUENCE, generator = "txn_seq")
    @SequenceGenerator(name = "txn_seq", sequenceName = "txn_seq", allocationSize = 50)
    private Long id;
    // 100,000 INSERTs = 2,000 round trips for ids + batched INSERTs
}
```

### How do you fix UUID index fragmentation?

Use a time-ordered identifier like UUIDv7 (Hibernate 6.2+) or ULID so inserts append near the end of the B-tree instead of scattering randomly.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import org.hibernate.annotations.UuidGenerator;

@Entity
public class Payment {

    // Time-ordered UUIDv7: inserts append, no fragmentation
    @Id
    @UuidGenerator(style = UuidGenerator.Style.TIME)
    private java.util.UUID id;

    private String fromAccount;
    private String toAccount;
    private java.math.BigDecimal amount;
}
```

```text
UUIDv7 vs UUIDv4:
- UUIDv4: random -> scatters across B-tree -> page splits -> fragmentation
- UUIDv7: timestamp prefix -> appends near end -> no splits -> fast inserts
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer defaults to SEQUENCE with allocationSize on PostgreSQL and Oracle because it batches well and the id is known before flush. They keep the allocationSize aligned with the actual sequence increment. They use UUID only when distributed generation is needed and choose UUIDv7 over UUIDv4. They know that with IDENTITY, Hibernate must flush an INSERT immediately on persist because it needs the generated id, while with SEQUENCE and UUID it can defer.

```yaml
# application.yml: enable JDBC batching (works with SEQUENCE, not IDENTITY)
spring:
  jpa:
    properties:
      hibernate:
        jdbc:
          batch_size: 50
        order_inserts: true
        order_updates: true
```
